The hypothesis is clear and the bug location is confirmed at lines 51-56. Now let me move to the IMPLEMENTATION phase.

The fix: When a default is provided and has a string/boolean type, the alias keys should also be added to `opts.string` or `opts.boolean` so the `toVal` function knows to coerce them to the right type.

Currently:
```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
    }
}
```

After fix:
```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        const arr = opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || [];
        arr.push(k);
        for (i = 0; i < opts.alias[k].length; i++) {
            arr.push(opts.alias[k][i]);
        }
    }
}
```

Wait, but I need to be careful. The variable `i` is reused. Let me use a separate variable or `forEach` to avoid side effects. Actually let me look at how `for...in` interacts with the `i` counter.

Actually `i` is used in the main parsing loop after this. Looking at the code:

```javascript
var i=0, j=0, idx=0, len=args.length;
```

The `i` is declared at the top. After this defaults block, `i` would be reset in the parsing loop. But within the loop, after `i++` in the inner block at line 78, it's used. The for loop over `args` resets `i` at line 60 (`for (i=0; i < len; i++)`).

So if I reuse `i` in the defaults block, then it gets reset to 0 in the parsing loop. So reusing `i` is fine for consistency, but let me use a cleaner approach.

Looking at the existing style, the code uses traditional for loops with reused counter variables. Let me follow the same style. Here's my proposed fix:

```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        arr = opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || [];
        arr = arr.concat(k);
        for (i=0; i < opts.alias[k].length; i++) {
            arr = arr.concat(opts.alias[k][i]);
        }
        opts[typeof opts.default[k]] = arr;
    }
}
```

Hmm, that's a bit verbose. Let me make it simpler with push:

```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
        for (i=0; i < opts.alias[k].length; i++) {
